## 1. Introduction
Cofactor engineering is an important strategy of metabolic engineering for achieving high yield and productivity of metabolites (Chemler et al., 2010). The generation of NADPH as reducing equivalent is often a limiting step for optimizing the production of many industrially important chemical products. Amino acids represent one of the largest classes of fermentation products whose syntheses are closely correlated with the availability of NADPH. As an example, the synthesis of 1 mol lysine requires 4 mol NADPH in Corynebacterium glutamicum. Over the past decades, different strategies have been tried to enhance the supply of NADPH for improved lysine production. Most of these metabolic engineering strategies focused on increasing the fluxes through pentose phosphate pathway (PPP), the main source of NADPH generation in C. glutamicum, by altering the specific enzyme concentrations (Becker et al., 2005). For example, overexpressions of the enzymes in PPP pathway, such as glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (Becker et al., 2007), 1, 6-bisphosphatase (Becker et al., 2005), and transketolase and transaldolase (Becker et al., 2011), led to enhanced fluxes towards PPP and obvious improvement of lysine production. Alternatively, destroying the phosphoglucose isomerase gene also redirected the metabolic flux from glycolysis towards PPP (Marx et al., 2003). The increased fluxes towards PPP, however, also resulted in the inevitable loss of carbon in substrate.
Reengineering new enzymes that can produce NADPH without the cost of carbon dissipation represents a new approach for NADPH regeneration. Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase (GAPDH) is an enzyme that catalyzes an inevitable step in the central metabolism of most industrially important sugars such as glucose, fructose and sucrose. During the glycolysis of $1\mathrm{mol}$ glucose, $2\mathrm{mol}$ of ${\tt N A D}(\mathrm{P})\mathrm{H}$ would be generated at this enzymatic reaction with the oxidation of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate to 1, 3- bisphosphoglycerate. Engineering the coenzyme specificity of (GAPDH) as a promising NADPH source is thus of considerable interest for the metabolic engineering of NADPH-dependent bioproduction systems such as lysine.
In a previous study, the NADP-dependent GAPDH (which accepts both NADP and NAD with preference of NADP) from Clostridium acetobutylicum has been used to replace the native NAD-dependent GAPDH in Escherichia coli, which significantly enhance the production of lycopene and $\varepsilon$ -caprolactone (Martínez et al., 2008). Takeno et al. (2010) also tried to replace the NAD-dependent GAPDH in C. glutamicum ATCC 13032 by a non-phosphorylating highly NADPspecific GAPDH from Streptococcus mutans for improving lysine production. The resulting strain, however, showed significant growth deficiency and was unable to utilize glucose. By using a long period of culture, a suppressor mutant was spontaneously isolated in the exponential phase which showed improved growth rate on glucose. The suppressor mutant produced larger amount of lysine as compared to those produced by the reference strain which owned the original gapA gene. Although the reason for the different observations is unclear, we believe that an appropriate tuning of the cofactor specificity and enzyme activity according to the cell physiology is important to both confer overproduction phenotype and to minimize the toxicity of reducing power perturbation.
During the past decade, a number of efforts have been paid to switch the coenzyme specificity either by directed evolution or rational design. Successful examples have been made for enzymes such as formate dehydrogenase (Andreadeli et al., 2008), mannitol 2-dehydrogenase (Bubner et al., 2008), alcohol dehydrogenase 8 (Rosell et al., 2003) and ketol-acid reductoisomerase (BrinkmannChen et al., 2013). Most of the previous studies were focused on the mutagenesis of the residues surrounding the cofactor binding sites in order to alter the size or electrostatic state of the binding pocket. However, a full reversal of coenzyme specificity without a significant loss of catalytically efficiency has been rarely achieved due to the fact that many residues contribute to binding and specificity of coenzyme.
In the present work, we attempted to systematically modify the coenzyme specificity of the native NAD-dependent GAPDH of C. glutamicum by rational protein design. The effect of cofactor alterations on cell metabolism and lysine production was assessed by wellcontrolled comparative fermentations and metabolic flux analysis. By carefully tuning the coenzyme specificity of GAPDH, we succeeded in increasing both lysine yield and productivity at the same time.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Molecular modeling
Homology models of C. glutamicum GAPDH were generated based on the structures of E. coli GAPDH (Protein Data Bank code 1GAD) and Bacillus Stearothermophilus GAPDH (Protein Data Bank code 1DBV and 4DBV) using the molecular modeling software Modeler (Sali and Blundell, 1993). Three residues within the active sites (D35-L36-T37) were subjected to in silico combinatorial mutagenesis (D35G, D35A, D35R, L36T, D35G/L36T, D35G/L36R and D35G/L36T/T37K). The three dimensional structures of in silico mutants were constructed by homology modeling using the program Modeler. The docking simulations of cofactor-GADPH complexes were conducted using AutoDock Vina tool (Morris et al., 1998; Trott and Olson, 2010). For each ligand, around 500 docking runs with default parameters were performed treating protein as rigid and the side chains of residue 35-36-37 and the ligand as flexible. The conformational clusters with lowest binding energy for each ligand were considered for further studies.
## 2.2. Bacterial strains and plasmids
Strains and plasmids used in this study are listed in Table 1. E. coli JM109 was routinely used for the construction of plasmids and
E. coli BL21-CodonPlus (DE3)–RIL cells (Stratagene) were used as the host for enzyme overexpression and purification. C. glutamicumppc was a lysine producing strain derived from the wildtype C. glutamicum ATCC 13032, containing a point mutation of Q298G in the lysC gene and a mutation of N917G in the ppc gene (Chen et al., 2011a, 2014).
The gapA gene encoding the wildtype NAD-dependent GAPDH was amplified by PCR from genomic DNA of C. glutamicum ATCC 13032 using primers $5^{\prime}$ -GAGCCTCATATGACCATTCGTGTTGGTATTAACG- ${\cdot}3^{\prime}$ and $5^{\prime}$ -TGTAGTCGACGAGCTTGGAAGCTACGAGC- ${\cdot}3^{\prime}$ (restriction sites are underlined). The DNA fragment was double-digested with NdeI and SalI, and cloned into plasmid pET-28a $(+)$ (Novagen), designated as pET-gapA. The point mutations in the gapA gene of pETgapA were introduced by using QuikChanges site-directed mutagenesis kits (Strategene) according to the standard protocol. The introduction of point mutations into the genome of C. glutamicum was achieved by a two-step homologous recombination process using the suicide vector pK18mobsacB (Schäfer et al., 1994) as described before (Chen et al., 2011a).
## 2.3. Protein expression and purification
GAPDH and its mutants were expressed in E. coli BL21- CodonPlus (DE3)–RIL cells (Stratagene) using the pET-gapA and its derived plasmids. The recombinant cells were firstly grown in LB media supplemented with $50\upmu\mathrm{g/ml}$ Kanamycin at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until the $\mathrm{OD}_{600}$ reached 0.6 and gene expression was induced by adding $0.1\ \mathrm{mM}$ isopropyl $\upbeta$ -D-thiogalactopyranoside (IPTG). The culture was continued for an additional $12{-}14\mathrm{h}$ at $20^{\circ}\mathsf C$ before the cells were harvested. The harvested cells were washed and suspended in the buffer of $50~\mathrm{mM}$ ${\sf N a}_{2}{\sf H P O}_{4}$ $\mathrm{\Phi}_{\mathrm{\tiny{pH}}}7.5\$ , $0.2\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA and $0.1~\mathrm{mM}$ dithiothreitol. Suspended cells were disrupted by sonication and centrifuged at ${10,000g}$ for $^{1\mathrm{h}}$ . The supernatant was purified by $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ -NTA column (GE Healthcare Bio-Sciences, Piscataway, NJ) to obtain the samples for GAPDH activity assay. The purity of the enzymes was checked by SDS-PAGE and the protein concentrations were quantified using a Bio-Rad protein assay kit (Bio-Rad Laboratories).
## 2.4. Enzyme assay
The enzyme activity of GAPDH was determined at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ by monitoring the absorbance change of ${\mathsf{N A D}}({\mathsf{P}}){\mathsf{H}}$ at $340\mathrm{nm}$ using a Multiskan spectrophotometer (Thermo Scientific, Germany). Initial rate measurements were carried out in $50~\mathrm{mM}$ triethanolamine hydrochloride buffer $\mathrm{(pH}~8.5)$ , $0.2\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA, $50~\mathrm{mM}$ ${\tt N a}_{2}{\mathrm{HP}}0_{4}$ , $1.5\:\mathrm{mM}$ glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (Clermont et al., 1993). Kinetic constants were obtained from non-linear regression data analysis and expressed as the mean $\pm{\sf S}.{\sf D}$ .
## 2.5. Fermentation and metabolic flux analysis
For the quantification of the profiles of C. glutamicum mutants, batch fermentations were carried out in $1.5\mathrm{L}$ bioreactors (DASGIP parallel bioreactor system, Jülich, Germany) with the working volume of $400~\mathrm{{mL}}$ The medium used was a defined minimal media containing $30{\ g\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ glucose, $20{\ g\mathrm{L}}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{NH}_{4})_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , $0.06\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}.2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $0.5\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{MgSO_{4}}.7\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $2{\mathrm{~g~L~}}^{-1}$ NaCl, $2\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{KH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4},$ $2{\mathrm{~g~L~}}^{-1}$ ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{HPO}}_{4}$ , $15\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ 3,4-dihydroxybenzoic acid, $0.5\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ biotin, $1.6\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ Thiamine-HCl, $3\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ nicotinic acid and 10 mL L1 of $100\times$ trace elements from Vallino and Stephanopoulos, (1993). For the labeled glucose experiments, $30\mathrm{g}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\left[1-^{13}\mathrm{C}\right]$ labeled glucose with $99\%$ atom purity (SigmaAldrich, Germany) was used instead of natural glucose. The fermentations were maintained at $30^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ pH 7.2, and aeration of 1 vvm. Dissolved oxygen was controlled at $30\%$ saturation by automatic control of impeller rpm in the DASGIP parallel bioreactorss.
Quantification of glucose, trehalose and organic acids were carried out by using High performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) (Kontron instruments, UK) involving separation on an Aminex HPX-87 H column at $60^{\circ}C$ with 0.005 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S0_{4}$ as the mobile phase with a flow rate of $0.6\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{min}$ , and detection via refractive index or by UV absorption at $210\mathrm{nm}$ . The extracellular amino acids were quantified by HPLC (Knauer, Germany) after derivatizing with 6- Aminoquinolyl-N-Hydroxysuccinimidyl carbamate (Waters AccQ. Flour Reagent kit, USA). Cell concentration was determined at an optical density of $660\mathrm{nm}$ and by dry biomass measurements.
The mass isotopomer fractions of the protein bound amino acids from the biomass were determined by gas chromatography– mass spectroscopy (GC–MS) as described by Becker et al. (2005) and Zamboni et al. (2009). The metabolic flux analysis was performed according to the model described by Becker et al. (2005) using the software package OpenFLUX (Quek et al., 2009).
## 3. Results
## 3.1. Probing the targeted residues determining the coenzyme specificity of GAPDH
The GAPDH of C. glutamicum encoded by the gapA gene was first overexpressed in E. coli. The purified GAPDH did not show any detectable activity toward NADP (Table 2), confirming that this canonical enzyme is a highly NAD-specific dehydrogenase (Omumasaba et al., 2004). The discrimination between NAD- and NADP-dependent dehydrogenases is the consequence of the overall binding pocket involving many structural and physicochemical factors (Bellamacina, 1996). To probe the potential residues affecting such process, we constructed a homology model of C. glutamicum GAPDH using the crystal structures of GAPDH from E. coli (Protein Data Bank code 1GAD) and B. stearothermophilus (Protein Data Bank code 1DBV and 4DBV) as template. The cofactor specificity of GAPDH from B. stearothermophilus has been previously studied by Branlant and coworkers and it was shown that at least three factors determine the coenzyme specificity: (1) the electrostatic repulsion between Asp and $2^{\prime}$ -phosphate of $\mathsf{N A D P}^{+}$ ; (2) steric hindrance; (3) structural determinants situated at subunit interfaces of the tetramer (Corbier et al., 1990; Clermont et al., 1993; Didierjean et al., 1997). As shown in Fig. 1A, the carboxylate group of Asp35 forms a network of hydrogen bonds with the $2^{\prime}$ and $3^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl groups of the adenosine ribose ring of NAD. This seems to be a critical factor to discriminate against the $2^{\prime}$ -phosphate group of NADP because of electrostatic repulsion between the carboxylate group and phosphate group. Multiplesequences alignment of NAD-dependent GAPDHs also showed that this Asp residue is highly conserved and mutated this residue to Ala could alter the cofactor specificity (Corbier et al., 1990; Clermont et al., 1993). The two successive residues, Leu36 and Thr37, are also considered as potential targets as they determine the flexibility of the loop between the second $\upbeta$ strand and the subsequent $\upalpha$ helix. Moreover, Pro192, a residue located at the subunit interface that was previously reported to be involved in coenzyme binding in B. stearothermophilus GAPDH, was also taken into account (Didierjean et al., 1997). In nature, the substitution of Pro to Ser is often observed in NADP-dependent GAPDH. All of these residues were subjected to in silico combinatorial mutagenesis and docking screening and 11 candidates are chosen for experimental characterization.
with NADP is still 3-fold lower than that with NAD. The NAD binding affinity of the D35G mutant is much lower than that of the wildtype enzyme as indicated from the $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{m}}$ value. This may due to the suppression of the hydrogen bonds between the Asp35 carboxylate and $2^{\prime}$ and $3^{\prime}$ -hydroxyl groups of the adenosine ribose ring of NAD. This point mutation, however, removes the potential electrostatic repulsion and steric hinderance and thus contributes to the increased binding affinity of NADP. The mutation of Leu36 to Thr also enabled GAPDH to accept NADP although the catalytic efficiency is very low. Interestingly, introducing a third mutation $\mathrm{T}37\mathrm{K}$ to the mutant D35G/L36T completely reversed the coenzyme specificity of the enzyme. The catalytic efficiency of the enzyme with NADP reached $4800\mathrm{mM}^{-1}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ which is about 10 fold higher than that with NAD. This enzyme activity is also comparable to the natural enzyme activity using NAD as cofactor. The combinatorial mutation of D35G/L36T/T37K, as indicated in the molecular docking model of Fig. 1B, provides a network of hydrogen bonds with the $2^{\prime}$ -phosphate group of NADP which may explain the reversion of the coenzyme preference of GAPDH.
The replacement of Pro192 to Ser benefited the binding affinity of both NAD and NADP (Table 2). The triple mutations of $\mathsf{D}35\mathsf{G}/\mathsf{L}36\mathsf{R}/$ P192S enable GAPDH to accept both NAD and NADP with similar catalytic efficiency. GAPDH with quadruple mutations of $\mathsf{D}35\mathsf{G}/\mathrm{L}36\mathrm{T}/$ T37K/P192S showed the highest catalytic efficiency with NADP while the catalytic efficiency with NAD also increases. The specificity constant $k_{c a t}/K_{m}$ of D35G/L36T/T37K/P192S mutant towards NADP, however, is only about 3-fold higher than that towards NAD.
3.2. Effect of the varied coenzyme specificity of GAPDH on the fermentation profiles of C. glutamicum
The three active GAPDH mutants with varied coenzyme specificity were integrated into the genome of C. glutamicum ppc strain by a two-step homologous recombination process. C. glutamicum ppc is a lysine producing strain derived from C. glutamicum ATCC 13032, with point mutations of Q298G in the lysC gene and N917G in the ppc gene which enable the deregulation of the feedback inhibition of aspartokinase and phosphoenolpyruvate carboxylase (Chen et al., 2011a). The corresponding gapA mutant strains were designated as C. glutamicum gapA M1 (with mutations of $\mathsf{D}35\mathsf{G}/$ L36T/T37K), gapA M2 (with mutations of D35G/L36R/P192S) and gapA M3 (with mutations of D35G/L36T/T37K/P192S).
The performance of the engineered C. glutamicum mutants was assessed in a well-equipped bioreactor system with four parallel bioreactors under controlled conditions with defined minimal medium. As shown in Fig. 2, all of the gapA mutants can grow normally under the tested conditions and showed improved lysine production compared to the control strain (Table 3). C. glutamicum gapA M1, which displayed the best reversal of cofactor specificity of GAPDH (indicated as $(k_{c a t}/K_{m})_{\mathrm{NADP}}/(k_{c a t}/K_{m})_{\mathrm{NAD}})$ , had similar growth and glucose consumption rate as those of the control strain but significantly improved lysine yield and productivity (about $20\%$ . The growth and glucose consumption rate of C. glutamicum gapA M2 and gapA M3 were lower than those of the control strain and gapA M1. Interestingly, C. glutamicum gapA M2, which has a similar catalytic efficiency towards NAD and NADP, produced the highest amount of lysine which was about $60\%$ higher than that of control strain. We did not detect significant amounts of byproducts except carbon dioxide. By considering lysine, carbon dioxide and biomass a carbon recovery of more than $90\%$ was obtained for all the fermentations. All of the gapA mutants showed a reduced carbon dioxide production as indicated from the carbon dioxide evolution rate (CER) (Fig. 2).
## 3.3. Metabolic flux analysis
To further characterize the effect of gapA mutations on the cell physiology and product formation, C-13 labeling experiments and metabolic flux analysis were carried out using the control strain and C. glutamicum gapA M2. The flux distributions during the exponential growth phase of the strains are shown in Fig. 3. Interestingly, although the flux to lysine synthesis was much higher in gapA M2 strain, the flux partitioning at the glucose-6- phosphate node was quite similar in both strains, indicating that the PPP pathway was not the main source of NADPH attributing to the increased lysine production. As expected, the flux to the TCA cycle in the gapA M2 strain was reduced by $10\%$ which was consistent with the results of CER measurement. The reduced carbon dissipation in the TCA cycle was redistributed to the net flux of anaplerotic pathway and enhanced lysine production. The reduced fluxes to the TCA cycle and the C4 decarboxylating pathway excluded the possibility that NADP-dependent isocitrate dehydrogenase and malic enzyme contributed to the increased NADPH essential for lysine overproduction. All of these evidences supported that the enhanced lysine production was mainly caused by the NADPH generated from the mutated GAPDH.
## 4. Discussion
The fast development of synthetic biology has enabled more efficient construction of new or improved pathways for the (Fillinger et al., 2000; Omumasaba et al., 2004). Takeno et al. (2010) tried to use the native NADP-dependent GAPDH encoded by gapB gene without success. As far as we known, all of the reported examples to utilize NADP-dependent GAPDH were based on exogenous genes. Here, by combining protein engineering and metabolic engineering, we reported for the first time the utilization of native GAPDH for generating a novel NADPH synthesis pathway and improving lysine production.
production of natural or non-natural compounds (Chen et al., 2010; Leonard et al., 2010; Vogt et al., 2014). Protein engineering plays important roles in synthetic biology for its special ability to design enzymes with new properties (Chen et al., 2011b, 2013; Geng et al., 2013) or functions (Leonard et al., 2010; Zhang et al., 2010). With respect to coenzyme generation, protein engineering could be used to design enzymes with altered coenzyme specificities, providing an alternative approach besides the classical metabolic engineering strategy.
The oxidation of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate to 1, 3-bisphosphoglycerate is an important reaction for the generation of reducing power in the glycolytic pathway. Since this reaction is common for the glycolytic pathway of sugars, it is an appealing target for the reengineering of NADPH generation for the synthesis of many industrially valuable compounds. In most of the bacteria, this step is catalyzed by strictly NAD-dependent GAPDH encoded by gapA gene. Although there exists another dual-coenzyme specific GAPDH in C. glutamicum, this enzyme is only active in gluconeogenesis
About $\sim25\%$ excess of NADPH is observed in gapA M3 strain. The excess of NADPH generation may inhibit the cell growth and glucose consumption. Interestingly, gapA M1 strain which has similar GAPDH enzyme kinetics as gapA M3, showed similar growth and metabolic flux distribution as the control strain. The reason for the difference between gapA M1 and M3 was unclear. We believe that the effect of the mutant GAPDH on the cellular metabolism is a strain-dependent event and a fine tuning of the overall metabolism is necessary. Further study on straindependent omics may be carried out to clarify the potential reason.
Since the GAPDH mutants in our study can accept both NAD and NADP, it is difficult to directly quantify the amount of NADPH generated from the enzymatic step catalyzed by GAPDH. Comparing the metabolic flux distribution between the control strain and C. glutamicum gapA M2 strain showed that the flux distribution at the node of glucose-6-phosphate was quite similar. Considering that the generation of $1\mathrm{mol}$ lysine requires $4\mathrm{mol}$ NADPH, the enhanced flux to PPP pathway can only contribute $26\%$ (calculated as $(2\times(54.0\mathrm{-}51.0))/(4\times(14.8\mathrm{-}9.2)))$ of the NADPH necessary for the increased lysine production (Fig. 3). Since the fluxes to the TCA cycle and C4 decarboxylating pathway in C. glutamicum gapA M2 strain were reduced, the NADP-dependent isocitrate dehydrogenase and malic enzyme were not the possible source for the enhanced NADPH generation. Thus, the NADPH generated from GAPDH may contribute the other $74\%$ of NADPH necessary for the increased lysine production.
The successful construction of an endogenous NADP-dependent GAPDH can be utilized for many industrially important metabolites production. The present study highlights the importance of protein engineering as a key strategy in de novo pathway design and metabolic engineering.
